WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.651
The jacket is not useful simply because a
utility pipe reaches the room.

2
00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:08.525
Compare the process need, the vessel
design assumptions and the

3
00:00:08.645 --> 00:00:10.544
available utility envelope.

4
00:00:10.664 --> 00:00:14.568
Our fictional process duty remains a
controlled input rather than

5
00:00:14.688 --> 00:00:16.075
an invented number.

6
00:00:16.195 --> 00:00:21.016
Consider flow, temperature, pressure and
capacity under relevant conditions,

7
00:00:21.136 --> 00:00:23.696
using appropriate engineering expertise.

8
00:00:23.816 --> 00:00:28.424
A utility qualification report and a
vessel design review answer different

9
00:00:28.544 --> 00:00:32.417
questions; their interface still needs a
compatibility assessment.

10
00:00:33.037 --> 00:00:37.133
An arrangement drawing should support safe
and practical operation,

11
00:00:37.253 --> 00:00:41.839
cleaning, inspection and maintenance
within the applicable facility design.

12
00:00:41.959 --> 00:00:45.541
Our sketch places an instrument behind a
vessel where removal

13
00:00:45.661 --> 00:00:46.950
may be blocked.

14
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:51.422
Ask whether the required access exists
after piping and adjacent

15
00:00:51.542 --> 00:00:53.099
equipment are installed.

16
00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:57.699
Do not treat empty space on a two-
dimensional drawing as proof of access.

17
00:00:57.819 --> 00:01:01.872
Engineering checks, dimensions or a
suitable model may be needed.

18
00:01:02.492 --> 00:01:06.780
A boundary can divide contracts without
dividing the technical risk.

19
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.423
Record who supplies the connector, who
defines its rating, who confirms

20
00:01:11.543 --> 00:01:14.807
compatibility and who accepts the
interface evidence.

21
00:01:14.927 --> 00:01:19.727
The fictional register assigns utility
conditions to site engineering and

22
00:01:19.847 --> 00:01:24.007
connection details to the vendor, with a
joint compatibility review.

23
00:01:24.127 --> 00:01:27.540
This prevents a gap where each party
assumes the other has

24
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.005
checked the connection.

25
00:01:29.125 --> 00:01:32.944
Responsibilities should match the site’s
actual organization.

26
00:01:33.564 --> 00:01:36.807
Suppose the utility schedule and vendor
drawing show

27
00:01:36.927 --> 00:01:38.738
different pressure assumptions.

28
00:01:38.858 --> 00:01:41.396
Stop treating the interface as resolved.

29
00:01:41.516 --> 00:01:45.612
Determine which values are correct and
assess the effect on design,

30
00:01:45.732 --> 00:01:48.036
protective measures and future tests.

31
00:01:48.156 --> 00:01:52.210
Do not choose the higher number merely to
make the documents agree.

32
00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:56.639
A controlled technical decision should
update the affected records and

33
00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:00.514
retain traceability to the requirement and
design basis.
